+# Contract: `logredact` command-line interface
+
+**Feature**: `001-redact-log-pii` | **Date**: 2026-11-13
+
+The CLI is the only interface this feature exposes. This document is the authoritative
+description of its surface; the redaction rules it applies are in
+[`redaction-rules.md`](./redaction-rules.md).
+
+---
+
+## Invocation
+
+```text
+python -m logredact [--count] [FILE ...]
+```
+
+Requires Python 3.11+ with `src/` on `PYTHONPATH`. No installation step.
+
+### Arguments
+
+| Argument | Cardinality | Meaning |
+|----------|-------------|---------|
+| `--count` | 0 or 1 | Print a redaction summary to stderr after all output. |
+| `FILE` | 0 or more | Input file to read, in the order given. |
+
+`--count` and `FILE` arguments may be interleaved in any order: `--count a.log b.log`,
+`a.log --count b.log`, and `a.log b.log --count` are equivalent (FR-001).
+
+`-h` / `--help` prints usage to stdout and exits 0 (argparse default; not a redaction run).
+
+### Input selection
+
+| Command line | Input read |
+|--------------|------------|
+| no FILE | standard input (FR-002) |
+| one or more FILE | each FILE in order; **standard input is not read** (FR-003) |
+| the same FILE twice | that file is read twice, producing its output twice |
+
+---
+
+## Streams
+
+| Stream | Content |
+|--------|---------|
+| stdin | Log text, read only when no FILE is given. |
+| stdout | Every input line, in input order, exactly once, with matches replaced. Nothing else — no banners, no progress. |
+| stderr | The `--count` summary, or a diagnostic. Nothing on a successful run without `--count` (FR-023). |
+
+**Encoding**: UTF-8 in and out, with undecodable input bytes round-tripped byte-for-byte.
+Behaviour does not vary with `LANG`, `LC_ALL`, or `PYTHONIOENCODING` (FR-005a, FR-005b).
+
+**Line fidelity**: line terminators are preserved exactly, including CRLF and a missing final
+newline. Text outside a match is emitted byte-for-byte (FR-004, FR-005).
+
+**Ordering**: stdout is flushed before anything is written to stderr, so the `--count` summary
+always follows the complete output even when both streams are redirected to the same file
+(FR-021).
+
+---
+
+## The `--count` summary
+
+Exact form, one line, to **stderr**:
+
+```text
+emails=N ips=N cards=N
+```
+
+- Always all three keys, always in this order, single-space separated, `0` when none (FR-022).
+- One summary per run, covering all sources together, written once after all output (FR-021).
+- Each `N` equals the number of replacements of that type actually made — equivalently, the
+  number of placeholders of that type present in the output (SC-004).
+- **Suppressed** when a read fails (FR-026) and when the output consumer closes early (FR-028).
+
+---
+
+## Exit status
+
+The tool exits with **0 or 2 and no other value** (SC-006).
+
+| Status | Condition |
+|--------|-----------|
+| `0` | Every input was read to the end (FR-024); or the output consumer closed early (FR-028). |
+| `2` | A named FILE could not be opened or read (FR-025); or the command line contained an argument the tool does not accept (FR-029). |
+
+---
+
+## Failure behaviour
+
+### Unreadable FILE (FR-025, FR-026, FR-027)
+
+Covers every reason the input cannot be read as text: it does not exist, permission is denied,
+it is a directory, or reading fails partway through.
+
+- stderr receives a diagnostic that **includes the filename exactly as written on the command
+  line** and states what went wrong, in the form `python -m logredact: <name>: <reason>`.
+- No stack trace is printed for any of these cases.
+- Processing **stops**; later FILE arguments are not opened.
+- The `--count` summary is **not** printed, so an incomplete run is never presented as complete.
+- Output already written for earlier files remains on stdout, valid and complete up to the point
+  of failure.
+- Exit status `2`.
+
+### Invalid command line (FR-029)
+
+- stderr receives a usage message that **names the offending argument**, e.g.:
+
+  ```text
+  usage: python -m logredact [--count] [FILE ...]
+  python -m logredact: error: unrecognized arguments: --redact-all
+  ```
+
+- No input is read and **stdout is empty** — no redacted output, no `--count` summary.
+- The unknown argument is never silently ignored nor reinterpreted as a filename.
+- Exit status `2`.
+
+### Output consumer closes early (FR-028)
+
+When stdout is closed before the tool finishes writing (e.g. `| head -5`):
+
+- Processing stops immediately.
+- **Nothing** is written to stderr: no diagnostic, no `BrokenPipeError` trace, no `--count`
+  summary.
+- Exit status `0`.
+
+---
+
+## Worked examples
+
+Each is directly executable; see [`../quickstart.md`](../quickstart.md).
+
+| # | Command | stdout | stderr | Status |
+|---|---------|--------|--------|--------|
+| 1 | `echo 'user jane@example.com from 10.0.0.7' \| python -m logredact` | `user [EMAIL] from [IP]` | *(empty)* | 0 |
+| 2 | `echo 'paid with 4111 1111 1111 1111, ref 1234567890123' \| python -m logredact` | `paid with [CARD], ref 1234567890123` | *(empty)* | 0 |
+| 3 | `echo 'login from user@1.2.3.4' \| python -m logredact --count` | `login from [EMAIL]` | `emails=1 ips=0 cards=0` | 0 |
+| 4 | `echo 'a@b.io 1.2.3.4' \| python -m logredact --count` | `[EMAIL] [IP]` | `emails=1 ips=1 cards=0` | 0 |
+| 5 | `echo '999.1.1.1' \| python -m logredact` | `999.1.1.1` | *(empty)* | 0 |
+| 6 | `python -m logredact a.log --count b.log` | redacted `a.log` then redacted `b.log` | one combined summary | 0 |
+| 7 | `python -m logredact missing.log` | *(empty)* | names `missing.log` | 2 |
+| 8 | `python -m logredact good.log missing.log` | redacted `good.log` | names `missing.log`, no summary | 2 |
+| 9 | `python -m logredact /tmp` | *(empty)* | names `/tmp`, no traceback | 2 |
+| 10 | `python -m logredact --redact-all` | *(empty)* | usage naming `--redact-all` | 2 |
+| 11 | `python -m logredact big.log \| head -5` | first 5 redacted lines | *(empty)* | 0 |
+
+---
+
+## Stability
+
+The following are part of the contract and may not change without a spec amendment: the
+placeholder strings, the summary format and its three key names, the `{0, 2}` exit-status set,
+the stream assignment of output vs. diagnostics, and the UTF-8 round-trip guarantee.
+
+## Internal API (not a public contract)
+
+`src/logredact/` exposes nothing to third parties; only `python -m logredact` is public. The
+module split described in `plan.md` exists for testability (Principle III) and may be
+reorganised freely as long as this contract holds.
