+# Contract: Redaction rules
+
+**Feature**: `001-redact-log-pii` | **Date**: 2026-11-13
+
+The precise recognition grammar the tool applies to each line. Every pattern and example below
+was executed and verified during Phase 0 (see [`../research.md`](../research.md) R-003, R-004).
+The command-line surface is in [`cli-contract.md`](./cli-contract.md).
+
+---
+
+## Scanning algorithm (FR-018, FR-019, FR-020)
+
+One left-to-right pass per line, over a single compiled alternation whose branch order encodes
+the precedence:
+
+```text
+EMAIL | IP | CARD
+```
+
+1. The match starting at the **earliest position** wins.
+2. Where several types could start at the same position, the **earlier branch** wins:
+   email → IPv4 → card.
+3. A matched candidate is then **validated**. If validation fails, the candidate's original text
+   is emitted unchanged and scanning resumes after it; nothing inside it is re-examined.
+4. Scanning resumes **after** the replaced (or restored) span, so matches never overlap and
+   inserted placeholders are never rescanned or counted.
+
+Placeholders contain no `@` and no digits, so input that already reads `[EMAIL]`, `[IP]`, or
+`[CARD]` passes through unchanged and uncounted.
+
+Matching is per line; **no match spans a line boundary**. All patterns are ASCII-only, so lone
+surrogates standing for undecodable bytes never participate in a match.
+
+---
+
+## 1. Email → `[EMAIL]`
+
+```text
+[A-Za-z0-9._%+-]+@(?:(?:[A-Za-z0-9-]+\.)+[A-Za-z]{2,}|\d{1,3}(?:\.\d{1,3}){3})
+```
+
+- **Local part**: one or more of letters, digits, `.`, `_`, `%`, `+`, `-` (FR-007).
+- **Domain**, either:
+  - dot-separated labels of letters, digits and hyphens, ending in a final label of **at least
+    two letters**; or
+  - four dot-separated groups of one to three digits (FR-007a).
+- **No validation step** — a syntactic match is always redacted. Numeric-domain groups are
+  deliberately **not** range-checked: the preceding `local-part@` already identifies the text as
+  an address (FR-007a).
+- Trailing punctuation is outside the match (FR-008).
+
+| Input | Output | Counted |
+|-------|--------|---------|
+| `jane@example.com` | `[EMAIL]` | emails +1 |
+| `jane.doe+test@mail.example.com` | `[EMAIL]` | emails +1 |
+| `jane@example.com, next` | `[EMAIL], next` | emails +1 |
+| `user@1.2.3.4` | `[EMAIL]` | emails +1 (**not** `user@[IP]`, not an ip count) |
+| `user@999.1.1.1` | `[EMAIL]` | emails +1 (no range check here) |
+| `jane@example.c` | unchanged | — (final label < 2 letters) |
+
+---
+
+## 2. IPv4 address → `[IP]`
+
+```text
+(?<!\d)(?<!\d\.)\d{1,3}(?:\.\d{1,3}){3}(?!\d)(?!\.\d)
+```
+
+- Four groups of one to three digits joined by single dots (FR-010).
+- **Validation**: every group's numeric value must be **0–255 inclusive**. If any group exceeds
+  255, the entire candidate is emitted unchanged — never partially (FR-011).
+- Leading zeros are accepted: `010.0.0.7` is an address.
+- The four lookarounds implement FR-012: not adjacent to a digit on either side, not preceded by
+  digit-and-dot, not followed by dot-and-digit.
+
+| Input | Output | Counted |
+|-------|--------|---------|
+| `10.0.0.7` | `[IP]` | ips +1 |
+| `0.0.0.0` / `255.255.255.255` | `[IP]` | ips +1 |
+| `010.0.0.7` | `[IP]` | ips +1 |
+| `from 10.0.0.7.` | `from [IP].` | ips +1 (full stop kept) |
+| `999.1.1.1` / `256.1.1.1` | unchanged | — |
+| `1.2.3.4.5` | unchanged **in full** | — |
+
+---
+
+## 3. Payment card number → `[CARD]`
+
+```text
+(?<!\d)\d++(?:[ -]\d++)*+
+```
+
+- A **maximal** run of digit groups joined by **single** separators, each one space or one
+  hyphen. Mixed separators are accepted (`4111-1111 1111-1111`).
+- The possessive quantifiers make the candidate non-backtrackable: the scanner takes the longest
+  run and judges that, rather than hunting for a shorter run inside it.
+- **Validation**, both must hold:
+  1. after stripping separators, the digit count is **13–19 inclusive** (FR-016); and
+  2. the digits pass the **Luhn checksum** (FR-014).
+- On failure the entire candidate is emitted unchanged, never partially redacted (FR-015,
+  FR-016). On success the whole candidate **including its separators** is replaced.
+- `(?<!\d)` plus the maximal run implement FR-017: no card is ever found inside a longer digit
+  run.
+
+| Input | Output | Why |
+|-------|--------|-----|
+| `4111111111111111` | `[CARD]` | 16 digits, Luhn passes |
+| `4111 1111 1111 1111` | `[CARD]` | separators stripped, then as above |
+| `4111-1111-1111-1111` | `[CARD]` | hyphens |
+| `4111-1111 1111-1111` | `[CARD]` | mixed separators |
+| `x4111111111111111y` | `x[CARD]y` | letters are not digits; FR-017 guards digits only |
+| `1234567890123` | unchanged | 13 digits, Luhn fails |
+| `123456789012` | unchanged | 12 digits, too short |
+| `12345678901234567890` | unchanged | 20 digits, too long |
+| `4111  1111 1111 1111` | unchanged | doubled separator splits it into `4111` and a 12-digit run |
+| `2026-11-13 10:00:00` | unchanged | candidate `2026-11-13` is 8 digits |
+
+### Luhn checksum
+
+Over the separator-stripped digit string: walking right to left, double every second digit,
+subtract 9 from any doubled value above 9, sum all values, and accept when the total is divisible
+by 10.
+
+### Known consequence of the maximal-run rule
+
+A card joined by a single separator to an adjacent number forms **one** larger candidate:
+
+- `code 7-4111 1111 1111 1111` — a 17-digit candidate that fails Luhn, so **nothing is
+  redacted**.
+- `id 42 4111 1111 1111 1111` — an 18-digit candidate that happens to pass Luhn, so the whole
+  span including `42` is replaced by `[CARD]`.
+
+This follows directly from FR-014/FR-015 as fixed by the spec's doubled-separator edge case.
+Searching sub-runs instead would contradict that case. Flagged for review; see `plan.md`.
+
+---
+
+## Combined examples
+
+| Input line | Output line | `--count` |
+|------------|-------------|-----------|
+| `user jane@example.com from 10.0.0.7` | `user [EMAIL] from [IP]` | `emails=1 ips=1 cards=0` |
+| `a@b.io 1.2.3.4` | `[EMAIL] [IP]` | `emails=1 ips=1 cards=0` |
+| `paid with 4111 1111 1111 1111, ref 1234567890123` | `paid with [CARD], ref 1234567890123` | `emails=0 ips=0 cards=1` |
+| `login from user@1.2.3.4` | `login from [EMAIL]` | `emails=1 ips=0 cards=0` |
+| `[EMAIL] [IP] [CARD] unchanged` | identical | `emails=0 ips=0 cards=0` |
+| `nothing to see here` | identical | `emails=0 ips=0 cards=0` |
+
+Counting rule: one increment per replacement actually made, so the same address three times
+counts three times. The three counts always equal the number of placeholders of each type in the
+output (SC-004).
